# call-wrong-arity.tflw — CHECK-ONLY FIXTURE (M87): a call passing the wrong number of arguments —
# demonstrates `tflw check`'s teaching diagnostic (TF038: checker, `checkCalls`, tflw M87 / review
# `A4-03`). The declaration is four lines above the call site and the checker still let this through
# until M87; the run died on the step with `action "create ticket" expects 2 argument(s), got 1`.
#
# TF038 is the *sound* half of call resolution, and it holds in places TF037 does not: tflw resolves
# actions before JS helpers, and an action name is unique across the whole registry (TF035 refuses a
# duplicate statically, `buildRegistry` refuses one at run time), so a name matching a declared
# action is that action in every frame — even inside another action's body, even alongside a `use`.
#
# Run explicitly (dot-directory, invalid syntax — see bad-keyword.tflw's header for why):
#   npx tflw check tests/.checkonly/call-wrong-arity.tflw

action create ticket(subject, body)
  api POST /tickets body { subject: {subject}, body: {body} }
  expect status equals 201
  capture body.id as id
  give id

test "calls a two-parameter action with one argument"
  let ticketId = create ticket("Only the subject")
